Solution:-
🧠 Intuition: The "Dictionary of Spells"
You are a wizard trying to transform a scroll (source) into a new text (target). You have a book of spells (original → changed), where each spell changes a specific word into another for a cost.
Unlike the previous version (Char → Char), we can now change chunks of text.
Challenge: There are infinite possible substrings. We can't map them all.
Solution: We ONLY care about the substrings mentioned in our spellbook.
Map: Assign a unique ID to every unique string in original and changed.
Graph: Calculate the cheapest way to turn any Spell ID into any other Spell ID (Floyd-Warshall).
DP: Walk through the source text. At every character, try applying every possible "Spell" (substring conversion) that starts there.

🎨 Visual Data Flow:-
Step 1: The Graph (Floyd-Warshall)
Strings: "ab", "xy", "pq"
Map: "ab"→0, "xy"→1, "pq"→2
Edges: 0→1 cost 5, 1→2 cost 3.
Result: We know turning "ab" → "pq" costs 5+3=8.
Step 2: The DP Transition (Trie Matching)
Source: a b c d ...
Target: x y z w ...
Current Index i:
Option A (Match): If source[i] == target[i], move to i+1 for free.
Option B (Spell): Look ahead.
Do source[i...k] and target[i...k] exist in our Trie?
Yes! They map to IDs u and v.
Cost to convert u→v is pre-calculated.
dp[k+1] = min(dp[k+1], dp[i] + graph[u][v]).
